      "message": "In order to be a bit more defensive regarding timing side channels, perhaps we should simplify this by making a two-element struct array that contains the results for each case so we can select by the boolean outcome of the safe_memcmp?\n\nSomething along these lines perhaps:\n\nvolatile struct {\n  uint32_t attempts;\n  int ret;\n  uint8_t secret[...];\n} results_table[2] \u003d {\n  { 0, PW_ERR_LOWENT_AUTH_FAILED, {} },\n  { 0, EC_SUCCESS, {} },\n};\n\nresults_table[FALSE].attempts \u003d max(leaf_data.pub.attempt_count.v + 1, UINT3_MAX);\nmemcpy(results_table[TRUE].secret, \u0026leaf_data.sec.high_entropy_secret);\n\n\n// ... later ...\n\nvolatile int decision \u003d safe_memcmp(request-\u003elow_entropy_secret,\n\t\t\tleaf_data.sec.low_entropy_secret,\n\t\t\tsizeof(request-\u003elow_entropy_secret)) ? 1 : 0;\n\n// now use results_table[decision] to set up response with pretty good confidence that we get constant-time code.\n\n\n\nBtw. can you also do due diligence to compile whatever final version we land on and check the assembly to make sure it looks right?\n\nAlso, we\u0027re assuming that there are no cache timing effects (i.e. the data for one case loading faster then the other case). I think that\u0027s a valid assumption on the hardware this runs on. Fully setting up the results table in advance will also guard against this risk somewhat as it\u0027ll give us high confidence *all* data will actually be in the cache before making the decision.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 479,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 497,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "48465188afa886efe6a381147ee8a452b9f88992",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
